  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Sunday: The trough that brought afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms to the mountain areas yesterday has begun retrograding \nwestward and off the PNW coast. This will result in southwesterly \nflow, which will aid in keeping PWATs 100-150 percent above normal \n(0.9-1.2\"). We will remain in this pattern regime through the \nweekend and into early next week as the low stays in place off the \ncoast. Incoming weak shortwaves paired with daytime heating and the \nadded moisture will create chances for thunderstorms each afternoon \nand evening. Tonight, there is a 20-40 percent chance of \nthunderstorms focused along the Cascades and the ID Panhandle, and \ncurrent radar does show thunderstorms moving towards the Cascades. \nMain impacts with these thunderstorms will be gusty outflow winds, \nlocalized heavy rainfall, and possible fire starts due to lightning. \nThese impacts will remain similar through the weekend. While fire \nconcerns are elevated due to lightning and gusty winds, the added \nmoisture will limit any major fire weather concerns. Chances for \nthunderstorms will be similar on Saturday, but will be further \nconfined to the Cascades and far SE Washington/Central ID. \nThunderstorm chances will be further limited on Saturday as the low \nmoves further off the coast. \n\nMonday through Friday: Monday through Tuesday, the trough begins to \nmove back towards the forecast area. This will bring more widespread \nchances of precipitation, though largest chances still remain \nconfined to the ID Panhandle. Thunderstorm chances continue through \nTuesday, then taper off as a warmer and drier air mass moves in. \nCurrently, cluster analysis and long-term models remain in \ndisagreement over whether we will continue seeing a cooler and \nwetter pattern or transition to a warmer and drier pattern. Roughly \nhalf of the clusters show much of the PNW in a drier and warmer \npattern to end next week, while the other half shows us continuing \nto see lower heights. This will depend on whether the low off the \nwest coast strengthens or dissipates. Overall: expect unsettled \nweather conditions to continue with chances of afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms in mountain areas through early next week. \nTemperatures will remain very close to or just below normal. /AS",
